/-!
# Original-source reductions for the constrained catalogue

The general original natural local-family branch is now excluded in
LocalNaturalHexadExclusion: the actual lifted hexad involution acts with
rank-one displacement on the whole original V, contradicting quadratic
ratio two. Thus natural K and their Borels lie in M_Z at both original
source primes, and the group/intersection source rows hold on the entire
eligible original local family. The additional C-component block clauses
and constrained source refinement are assembled in SourceAssembly, retaining
the independent whole-core A₆ reconstruction and original 15.1.14 residual
as explicit inputs. Their discharge remains necessary for source completion.

The local-family opening of 15.1.20 is available for every K in L(G₁,S).
For an ambient C-component, C.2.4 now gives an actual rank-one block or
genuine normalization by Q₁. The full-Sylow branch is proved to be a
chi-zero block, with its solvable A₃ possibility excluded. In the normalized
case, C.2.7 gives the star-group catalogue and central comparison kernel.
Both the pre-exclusion and exchanged source interfaces use this reduction;
no exchanged selection is needed at prime five.

For every constrained member of the fixed local family, the original
central-involution span gives Thompson normalization. The source normalizer
K N_S(K) then gives a reduced FF-module and K=[K,J(S)]. The
proved B.5.1 classifier retains its full action clauses and actual image
group list. No ambient C-component hypothesis is imposed on that member.

The A₅ block now has its literal Borel and centralizer identified for every
eligible local-family member. Its chosen-component application supplies the
full rank-one alternative, including Thompson movement. For natural L₂ blocks,
the literal quotient now has a common Sylow/Borel matrix marking, M_Z∩K lies
in the Borel, its two-core is S∩K, and N_{M_Z}(K) preserves the Borel.

Original 15.1.19(2) now controls every M_Z-invariant characteristic-two
subgroup of G₁ equal to its lower mixed core. The proof uses the solvable
A₃ decomposition and original residual-normalizer bound. This supplies the
control step for the orbit Borel in 15.1.20. That orbit now has characteristic
two and a normal Sylow subgroup for every short local-family member. For the
selected C-component, its full M_Z-invariance is proved, so reverse containment
identifies the natural L₂ Borel. The original and exchanged applications now
give the complete rank-one alternative in the full-Sylow branch. Conversely,
that rank-one block condition now forces S_L≤Q₁. The new disjoint reduction
therefore excludes both natural L₂ and A₅ blocks from the remaining C.2.7
branch, on the same original and exchanged data.

The FF normalizer action now also has its literal central comparison from
K/O₂(K), whose kernel has order one or three. The induced K/O₂,Z(K)
isomorphism retains the original map. The marked intersection has a proper
image containing the original Sylow image, with exactly the original marked
intersection as its full preimage. A cubic image center forces the core map
to be an isomorphism.

The original local-family normalizer now retains its two-residual K and
its seed inside the full local omega-center span. Natural-action alpha
decomposition identifies the moved modules and action kernels. An actual
rank-one image supplies minimal-parabolic structure, and the natural block
application uses only Baumann characteristic-normalizer control. Its
conditional interface retains Baumann normalization as an input. The original B.5.1 action now
supplies the natural SL₂ and S₅/A₅ rows, including all coordinate and pair
transports. Both literal quotient forms give nonblock placement under Baumann
normalization. LocalSeparation now discharges that normalization input for
every eligible member of the original local family.

The rank-one fixed-witness step now retains the actual normalizer module,
its moved subgroup, and the original central kernel in both natural and
permutation branches. Its noncentral vector lies in α(S), without a block
or Baumann-normalization premise. The common K join S action now supplies
the complete B.5 classification on its offender-generated subgroup. Distinct
component images occupy opposite supports; perfectness removes the global
fixed-kernel ambiguity. Normalization reflects through the original two-core
quotient, proving literal moved-support separation and unconditional rank-one
nonblock placement in M_Z. Its original-image form also reconstructs a
chi-block directly from a rank-one normalizer image, retaining the central
comparison kernel. The actual G₂ and triality residuals now contradict the
nonblock theorem, excluding both higher-field G₂ rows (including central
G₂(4) covers) and the higher-field triality row before prime exclusion.

The constrained higher-field G₂ row is now excluded as well. Both literal
parabolic residuals are constructed for every original local-family member
with G₂ core quotient. A solvable-kernel generation theorem handles its
noncentral two-core, and the G₂ multiplier identifies the original FF image
with that core quotient, including field size four. The actual original-marker
and exchanged-source applications preserve their original selection data.

The natural and doubled-natural SL₃ source rows now have field degree one
for every eligible original local-family member. A split odd-prime rank bound
proves S-normalization, and the actual moved-section intertwiners preserve both
flag types. Both literal residuals therefore belong to L(G₁,S); nonblock
placement and generation exclude every higher field, including four elements.
The actual B.5 classifier, original marker and exchanged source consume this
argument without extra parabolic or membership premises.

The two actual SL₃/Sp₄ parabolic residuals now have proved rank-one core
quotients and characteristic-two generalized Fitting subgroups, and generate
the original K. The residual construction is identified both in K N_S(K)
and in K, and the multiplier calculations remove the possible extra central
kernel of the classical FF images, including SL₃(4).

For the selected component, the higher-field Sp₄ row is now excluded in
both the FF normalizer image and the C.2.7 star image. Odd rank proves
S-normalization, hence Q₁-normalization. The actual C.2.7 marked parabolic
fixes the diagram, and a common Sylow marking makes both parabolic residuals
members of the original local family. The proved nonblock theorem places
both in M_Z, contradicting their generation of L. The original-marker and
exchanged-source applications retain their selection data, including field
size four. The linear-three residual memberships and both natural source-row
exclusions are now supplied by LinearMembership and HigherLinear.

ReducedModels extracts the selected FF group catalogue after all these
higher-field exclusions. It retains actual isomorphisms for the rank-one,
binary SL₃, derived binary G₂, A₅--A₈ and SL₅(2) rows, and both the center
and central quotient of the cubic A₆ cover. Both original primes and the
later exchanged selection use this same reduced catalogue.

This is a reduction toward the catalogue, not its exhaustiveness theorem.
The general local-family Borel placement, block reconstruction and parabolic clauses must still be proved before
ConstrainedAlternatives can be supplied to the final constrained quotient assembly.

The surviving binary SL₄(2) and SL₅(2) FF rows now identify the literal
two-core quotient through the original comparison map. Both original
intersections are proper flag parabolics under that same isomorphism.
BinaryGeneration now constructs the interior and endpoint parabolics above
that same quotient. They are proper S-invariant flag subgroups, have
characteristic two, and generate L. The endpoint retains its intrinsic
S₃ × S₃ quotient, and the interior image is the centralizer of the quotient
Sylow center. BinaryExtensions now proves characteristic two and the product-or-wreath
quotient for the literal endpoint joined with N_S(L)=S. It retains the
same pair and marking in both original-prime and exchanged consumers.
BinaryNodes now identifies the actual node action through the same two-core
quotient. Inner action preserves every partial flag; graph action restricts
both original intersections to S_L and the same interior/endpoint generating
pair. The interior and, in the graph branch, endpoint retain their exact
full-preimage markings. BinaryWreath now selects the literal S₃ ≀ C₂ endpoint
quotient whenever the original action contains a graph actor. Equal orders
of the core quotients would force invariance of the first endpoint node,
contradicting its actual graph image. Both original primes and the exchanged
consumer retain the same quotient marking and pair. BinarySourceParabolics
also excludes an A₃-product two-residual for the degree-five graph endpoint:
its literal model image has a nonidentity sixth power, whereas every central
product of A₃ blocks satisfies the sixth-power identity. That endpoint now
satisfies every clause of the original source parabolic family.
BinaryNodePlacement now supplies the literal S₃ quotients, A₃-product
exclusions and full source-family membership for both degree-five interior
nodes when the Sylow action is inner. The original same-pair placement puts
their join, the constructed interior, inside the join of the original
intersections. In the graph branch, that placement and the node alternatives
identify the endpoint with one of the two original intersections. Both source
primes, the original marking, and the exchanged node-membership consumer are
retained.
BinaryInnerPlacement now supplies the full source family for every node
under inner Sylow action in both binary dimensions. The endpoint residuals
have at least sixteen elements, contradicting the proved bound of twelve
for an A₃ product with only one factor of three in its order. Full-preimage
generation and original same-pair placement then prove
(M ∩ L) join M_L = L, giving the actual linear ConstrainedAlternatives
branch. The original-marker export retains both source primes and the
compatible quotient marking; the exchanged export retains every inner node.
BinaryGraphFive completes the degree-five graph branch. The literal interior
Sylow extension has Aut(GL₃(2)) two-core quotient, so its reduced original-seed
module and the proved FF exclusion place the interior in M or M_Z. Together
with the already placed endpoint this proves generation and the full
degree-five linear alternative for both original primes. The exchanged
consumer retains the same interior quotient and placement. BinaryGraphFour now completes the
degree-four graph branch: the interior is the middle node, while the
endpoint A₃-product exclusion uses the full component’s characteristic two
and a strict residual-size increase over its model image. Original placement
then proves generation. Both binary degrees now supply the full linear
ConstrainedAlternatives branch with their original quotient markings.
RankOneImage now reconstructs the natural L₂ or genuine A₅ block from the
actual rank-one FF image, giving the complete rank-one alternative for the
selected component. G2Block reconstructs the whole G₂ block from its FF
image through the original core quotient and the actual M_Z odd-index
witness. Its positive parabolic and centralizer clauses remain to be proved.
OrdinaryApplications discharges the exact RankOneNonblockContainment used
by ordinary refinement on the full local family; the quintic Ordinary22
catalogue and the cubic Rudvalis remainder now have no nonblock callback.
The remaining small-block clauses and general-family Borel placement still
precede catalogue exhaustiveness.

SmallAlternating now supplies the literal central quotient and S-normalization
for the A₆/A₇ FF rows. It constructs the actual C.2.7 input and identifies
the FF and star central quotients through their original maps. Both six-point
rows, including the cubic cover, supply every MS-pair hypothesis on LQ₁.
The A₆ whole-core reconstruction remains explicit for its existing producer;
its natural and exceptional outcomes are both retained. Original-marker and
exchanged applications preserve the source primes and plane sizes.

SevenBlock now reconstructs the whole ordinary or exceptional A₇ block
from the actual normalizer action on the full original local family.
The original Baumann control excludes the normal-characteristic alternative.
Both block types make the two-core quotient centerless, so the original FF
map supplies a compatible A₇ quotient marking. Original-marker and exchanged
applications retain their selection and primes. SevenCentralizer now identifies
the exceptional fixed-line centralizer with the literal M_Z intersection,
with index 15, on the full local family. The two-core of its GL₃(2) image
is trivial, forcing Q₁ to centralize the exceptional layer in the selected
component. The actual
C.2.7 Thompson condition then excludes that row, leaving the ordinary
A₇ block with its compatible original quotient marking. SevenWeightTwo
now constructs a nonzero N_S(L)-fixed block vector, identifies M_Z ∩ L
with its maximal centralizer, and proves its weight is two using the
literal Q₁-normalizer cubic-rank bound and C.2.7 Thompson condition.
Original-marker and exchanged exports preserve the same quotient marking.
SevenPartition.Membership now proves every source-parabolic condition for
the literal three-pair preimage, including normalization, the S₃ quotient
and the A₃-product exclusion. Original placement puts it in M ∩ L, and
generation completes the A₇ catalogue constructor at both source primes,
with the prescribed quotient marking and an actual-marker application.
The exchanged application retains the same source-family construction.

BinaryThreeInput now identifies the actual binary-three core and star
quotients through the original FF map. Their order excludes the rank-one
blocks, so C.2.4 gives Q₁-normalization and every MS-pair condition on LQ₁.
SmallRemainder consumes all completed branches of the reduced FF catalogue.
It leaves only the binary-three and six-point central-quotient rows, with
their genuine C.2.7 input, MS pair and elementwise quotient markings.
Both original source primes and actual-marker applications are retained;
the binary-three input also has its later exchanged application.
BinaryThreeInterval now constructs the full literal Sylow-overgroup interval
for every binary-three local member. Its two maximal parabolics have index
seven, characteristic two and S₃ two-core quotients; they intersect in S∩K
and generate K. Their O^{3′} residuals generate them with S∩K and escape
the opposite parabolic. Both original intersections lie in that interval,
with the original FF marking retained. Their strictness above S_L and
mutual distinction give the actual maximal-parabolic, generation and
residual-escape clauses. BinaryThreePlacement now discharges all three
position facts. A normal Sylow orbit excludes M_L=S_L, and the fixed
maximal member forces N_S(L) to fix the other parabolic. Strict residual
size excludes A₃ products, so original source placement identifies the
intersections with the opposite maximal members and gives generation and
literal O^{3′} escape. Both source primes, original quotient markings and
the later exchanged family application are retained. BinaryThreeBlock now
reconstructs the whole natural L₃ block. Its faithful omega-center has order
two; the fixed-line stabilizer and central-kernel lift identify M_L with
C_L(Z), and residual escape gives the literal S-centralizer identity. The
complete L₃ alternative retains both marked parabolics and connects to the
existing exchanged-source exclusion. SixRemainder consumes this constructor,
leaving only the ordinary or cubic A₆ MS row in the reduced FF catalogue.
SixGeometry and SixParabolics now prove the ordinary A₆ block's faithful
omega-center order, literal seed centralizer, opposite maximal parabolics,
source-family membership and residual-centralizer identity. The original
FF quotient marking simultaneously identifies both parabolics, and the
exchanged-source obstruction consumes the resulting centralizer identity.
SixApplications removes this ordinary branch conditional on the independently
owned whole-core reconstruction, retaining the exceptional cubic block and
its original MS marking. ExceptionalSixSeed extracts a nonzero fixed vector
from the original central-involution seed for every exceptional member of
the full local family. It detects the full central kernel of the literal
FF map, retaining the cubic center and the entire two-core quotient.
Thus an ordinary A₆ FF image reconstructs only as the ordinary block, giving
its catalogue alternative and the later exchanged exclusion. The cubic row is now excluded by its actual parabolic offender: the original
seed centralizer normalizes Q₁, forcing J(Q₁) into O₂(KQ₁), contrary to C.2.7.
The selected constrained catalogue follows conditional on whole-core A₆
reconstruction and the earlier residual input. General local-family Borel
placement and full seven-row source assembly remain open.

LocalNaturalSylow proves S-normalization of every escaping natural local
member using the original prime residual and commutation of distinct blocks.
Its orbit Borel is the single original Borel, and both the M_Z-intersection
and Q₁-normalizer inside K S are S. The actual-marker application retains
the cubic field-four restriction. M_Z-invariance and the general natural
exclusion still require proof.

LocalNaturalMovingPlane places the entire original residual displacement
in the natural block's Sylow-fixed U-layer and proves its order is exactly
four. Thus markers with moving plane of order sixteen have no escaping
natural local members, and their full local-family group and intersection
rows are assembled at both source primes. The same cubic order-four plane
is retained in the separate exchanged application. The remaining general
natural exclusion has moving plane of order four.

The same module now identifies the centralizers in K of V and its original
moving plane with S intersect K, and both normalizers with the natural
Borel. It also constructs the entire normal closure of V in K S as
V joined with U(K), proves it elementary abelian, and places it in the
literal O₂(K S). Original-marker and exchanged applications retain the
same planes and full central attachment. This local two-core is not
identified with Q₁; the general cubic escape still needs exclusion.

Source: Aschbacher--Smith 15.1.20(2) and 15.1.22, printed pp.1094--1100.
The two actual G₂ parabolic preimages now satisfy the whole source family,
including the A₃-product residual exclusions. Same-pair placement gives
generation by the original intersections and escape of their literal
three-residual. The original quotient marking, both source primes and
exchanged applications are retained. The omega-center order bound and
positive G₂ centralizer identity remain to be proved.
-/

